WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.945
Four tail risks now threaten a self-reinforcing downward spiral.

3
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:07.194
Today is November 02, 2026.

4
00:00:05.895 --> 00:00:13.873
Lagging artificial intelligence monetization could force cloud giants to cut chip orders, widening corporate credit spreads further.

5
00:00:07.194 --> 00:00:14.829
Overnight, the S and P, Nasdaq, and Dow steadied after plunging on high Treasury yields and Fed rate fears.

6
00:00:13.873 --> 00:00:20.822
Meanwhile, Middle East shipping disruptions in the Strait of Hormuz could spike oil prices and reignite inflation.

7
00:00:14.829 --> 00:00:23.481
Last week began with euphoria over artificial intelligence compute and memory supercycles, then reversed into a broad cross-asset selloff.

8
00:00:20.822 --> 00:00:27.758
Combined with a stronger dollar and rising yields, tighter liquidity could trigger forced selling across stocks and gold.

9
00:00:23.481 --> 00:00:31.398
Equities, semiconductors, and gold fell together as the VIX volatility index spiked to its highest level since April.

10
00:00:27.758 --> 00:00:33.101
This has sparked a fierce clash between bulls and bears in semiconductors and memory.

11
00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:40.393
Institutional capital is rotating out of volatile tech into utilities, consumer staples, and traditional sixty-forty stock-and-bond portfolios.

12
00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:39.596
Bears are dumping chip stocks indiscriminately over runaway capital expenditures and high discount rates.

13
00:00:39.596 --> 00:00:45.552
Bulls counter that the thirty-seven percent plunge in memory chipmakers is an emotional overreaction.

14
00:00:40.393 --> 00:00:48.309
The equal-weight S and P five hundred just posted its strongest monthly outperformance over cap-weighted benchmarks since two thousand nine.

15
00:00:45.552 --> 00:00:51.336
Global memory shortages remain structural and are projected to persist through two thousand twenty-eight.

16
00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:56.177
Retail traders defied the rout, pouring a record seven billion dollars into triple-leveraged semiconductor fund S O X L.

17
00:00:51.336 --> 00:00:58.983
With over one hundred thirty S and P five hundred companies reporting this week, positioning requires strict cash-flow discipline.

18
00:00:56.177 --> 00:01:02.966
They also injected two billion dollars into triple-leveraged Nasdaq fund T Q Q Q to buy the dip.

19
00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:05.552
Intel remains a clear sell on any bounce after plunging twenty-eight point five eight percent in a single day.

20
00:01:02.966 --> 00:01:08.701
Weekend shocks across Europe and cryptocurrency markets added fresh macro uncertainty.

21
00:01:05.552 --> 00:01:14.020
That marked its worst session in fifty years, driven by weak personal computer demand, factory mishaps, and collapsing cash flow.

22
00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:17.206
Poland faces a constitutional crisis over judicial appointments, sparking mass protests in Warsaw that threaten fiscal and defense budgets.

23
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:28.052
With Intel's credit default swaps at record highs, investors should exit and monitor broad chip benchmark S O X X. Amazon also faces profit-taking pressure despite thirty-nine percent revenue growth in Amazon Web Services.

24
00:01:17.206 --> 00:01:26.226
In Germany, youth protests erupted across thirty cities against high living costs, housing shortages, and mandatory military conscription.

25
00:01:26.226 --> 00:01:33.836
Storefront looting in Frankfurt and continued street clashes in France are weighing heavily on the euro and European sovereign bonds.

26
00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:34.045
Doubling quarterly capital expenditures pushed Amazon's free cash flow into a deep deficit.

27
00:01:33.836 --> 00:01:41.765
In crypto, Binance covered ninety percent of recent hack losses, fully restoring Bitcoin and Ethereum user balances.

28
00:01:34.045 --> 00:01:42.243
Until cash flow stabilizes, institutions favor trimming cloud-heavy Q Q Q in favor of equal-weight S P Y.

29
00:01:41.765 --> 00:01:49.094
The exchange will tap its safety fund for full reimbursement, though security fears keep crypto volatility elevated.

30
00:01:42.243 --> 00:01:50.858
Conversely, memory leaders Samsung Electronics and S K Hynix offer compelling contrarian buy setups after falling thirty-seven percent.

31
00:01:49.094 --> 00:01:57.206
Two major macro catalysts dominate this week: the United States midterm elections and Friday's October nonfarm payrolls report.

32
00:01:50.858 --> 00:01:57.672
Both trade at historic lows of two to three times two thousand twenty-seven earnings with massive cash reserves.

33
00:01:57.206 --> 00:02:02.648
Markets have already priced in the downside risk of a Democratic sweep across Congress.

34
00:01:57.672 --> 00:02:04.143
Bulls are accumulating memory leaders here, provided the midterm elections avoid a full Democratic sweep.

35
00:02:02.648 --> 00:02:08.395
Citi notes that if Republicans hold the Senate, equities could stage a year-end relief rally.

36
00:02:04.143 --> 00:02:13.003
Finally, earnings from Apple, A M D, Palantir, and Qualcomm will test whether semiconductor and hardware sentiment can stabilize.

37
00:02:08.395 --> 00:02:17.488
Because Senate committees control financial regulation and Federal Reserve nominations, a split Congress favors buying healthcare against financial stocks.

38
00:02:13.003 --> 00:02:20.233
Apple's new chief executive must prove that Services growth and China resilience can offset broader hardware weakness.

39
00:02:17.488 --> 00:02:24.008
A Democratic sweep would pressure sentiment and bring forward fears of corporate tax hikes in two thousand twenty-eight.

40
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:24.241
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

41
00:02:24.008 --> 00:02:32.182
Democratic governor wins in Wisconsin, Michigan, and Nevada would also tighten environmental reviews and slow data center construction.

42
00:02:24.241 --> 00:02:28.407
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.

43
00:02:32.182 --> 00:02:40.172
On Friday, economists expect October nonfarm payrolls to rebound to sixty-five thousand from twenty-nine thousand previously.

44
00:02:40.172 --> 00:02:44.572
The unemployment rate is projected to hold at four point two percent.
